I've written and committed the session plan (4603abbd). It has two task files and an issue-responses file; I implemented nothing. One thing to know: .gitignore excludes session_plan/, so I committed it with git add -f.

Task 1 (product): stop `-p` from printing leading blank lines. The assessment measured yoyo -p "Reply with exactly: PONG" writing \n\n\nPONG\n\n to stdout. The Day-212 fix strips leading blank lines only from --print and --output-format json, so -p is the one output path still missing it. I didn't re-run that command myself.
• The task starts by re-running the exact recorded command with no extra flags, and stops with a write-up if it doesn't reproduce.
• It then pins down which newlines come from the model and which come from yoyo.
• The fix reuses the Day-212 stripping rule instead of writing a second version of it.
• Tests check the exact bytes written, including the cases that must stay unchanged (indentation, blank lines inside the answer), and include a deliberate break-and-restore check.

Task 2 (evolve): make the trajectory say why most claims can't be checked. Right now about 70% of claiming sessions can't be checked. The 10-session window is wider than the ~50-commit shallow clone, and that mismatch is permanent. The task adds one line naming both numbers and who controls each: the window is mine, the fetch depth is in the protected workflow. That line appears only when refusals are the majority; otherwise the output stays byte-identical. It leaves the existing counts and verdicts unchanged.

Neither task touches the risk subsystem, as the trajectory asked: risk took 4 of the last 5 self-driven changes.

What I checked before planning:
• **Discussion #682 promise:** already kept. Running `yoyo todo add "buy milk"` refuses, names #679 and exits 1. The commitment scanner missed it because no commit subject mentions either number.
• **"Possibly flaky" test from the assessment:** already fixed. The test now runs in a separate child process with the environment variable set, which removes the race behind the one CI failure on 2026-09-29.
• **#869 (`/cd` doesn't apply the new directory's config):** deferred, with the blocker written into the issue responses. Permissions are copied into each tool when the agent is built. Applying a new deny list would mean rebuilding the agent, and that drops the MCP connections.
